    <!-- Import guide -->
    <string name="import_guide">How to import recipes</string>
    <string name="guide_intro">Recipes come in from links, shared text, photos and screenshots. Whatever is found opens in the editor, so you can fix what was misread before saving.</string>
    <string name="guide_instagram_title">Instagram</string>
    <string name="guide_instagram">Tap the three dots on the post or reel, choose Copy link, then paste it into a new recipe. The caption gives the ingredients and steps; the picture or the video’s thumbnail becomes the photo. When the caption holds no recipe, the app listens to the video and writes the recipe down; that takes a minute and needs internet.</string>
    <string name="guide_tiktok_title">TikTok</string>
    <string name="guide_tiktok">Tap Share, then Copy link, and paste it into a new recipe. The caption is read, and when it holds no recipe the video is listened to. When the recipe sits in the comments, copy that text.</string>
    <string name="guide_facebook_title">Facebook</string>
    <string name="guide_facebook">Open the post, tap Share, then Copy link. Posts in private groups cannot be read: copy their text and share it to Cookbook Anything instead.</string>
    <string name="guide_youtube_title">YouTube</string>
    <string name="guide_youtube">Share the video’s link. Its description is read; when the recipe is in a pinned comment, copy and paste that.</string>
    <string name="guide_web_title">Recipe websites</string>
    <string name="guide_web">Share the page from the browser to Cookbook Anything, or paste the link. Most recipe sites give the exact ingredients, steps, times and photo.</string>
    <string name="guide_photo_title">Photos and screenshots</string>
    <string name="guide_photo">Photograph a cookbook page or a handwritten card, or share screenshots from your gallery; several photos are read as one recipe. Keep the text sharp and the page straight.</string>
    <string name="guide_text_title">Typing or pasting</string>
    <string name="guide_text">Write one ingredient per line, starting with the amount, for example “200 g flour”. A line ending with a colon, such as “For the sauce:”, becomes a heading. The app then scales, converts and groups the shopping list on its own.</string>

